 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Multi-view Inconsistency:** 2D instance masks from existing methods are generally multi-view inconsistent, as the same object may be assigned different instance IDs in different views [S1]. Instance-NeRF addresses this by projecting 3D masks to 2D to match instances across views [S1].
-*   **Ambiguity with Overlapping Objects:** Unlike Panoptic NeRF, which uses 3D bounding volumes as priors and cannot resolve ambiguity when objects of the same class have overlapping bounding volumes, Instance-NeRF does not rely on 3D priors [S1].
+*   **Multi-view Inconsistency:** 2D instance masks from external models often assign different IDs to the same object across different views. Instance-NeRF explicitly handles this via the matching/refining stage rather than ignoring it or relying on 3D priors [S1].
+*   **Overlapping Objects:** Unlike Panoptic NeRF, which uses 3D bounding volumes as priors, Instance-NeRF does not rely on such priors. This avoids ambiguity when objects of the same class have overlapping bounding volumes, a limitation of Panoptic NeRF [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
 *   **Time Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
 *   **Memory Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
-*   **Measured Performance:** Not given in the sources.
-*   **Strengths:**
-    *   One of the first learning-based approaches to generate 3D instance segmentation in NeRF without using ground-truth segmentation as input [S1].
-    *   Does not rely on 3D priors to address multi-view inconsistency, avoiding ambiguity when objects of the same class have overlapping bounding volumes [S1].
-    *   Can produce satisfactory 3D object masks on complex scenes during test time [S1].
-*   **Limitations:**
-    *   Requires a pre-trained NeRF model [S1].
-    *   Relies on existing panoptic segmentation models (e.g., Mask2Former) for 2D mask generation and refinement [S1].
-*   **When to use:** When 3D instance segmentation is required for a scene represented by a NeRF, especially in complex scenes with overlapping objects of the same class, and ground-truth 3D segmentation is unavailable.
-*   **When not to use:** When 2D semantic segmentation is sufficient, or when 3D bounding volume priors are available and preferred.
+*   **Strengths:** One of the first learning-based approaches for 3D instance segmentation in NeRF without requiring ground-truth 3D segmentation input. It produces satisfactory 3D object masks on complex scenes and resolves multi-view instance ID inconsistency without 3D bounding volume priors [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Performance may vary on simple scenes with limited objects, with room for quality improvement and generalization [S1]. Relies on external panoptic segmentation models (Mask2Former, CascadePSP) for supervision, which may propagate errors if those models fail [S1].
+*   **When to use:** When 3D instance segmentation is required for a scene represented by a NeRF, and multi-view consistency of instance IDs is critical, especially in scenes with overlapping objects of the same class.
+*   **When not to use:** When ground-truth 3D segmentation is available and a simpler supervised approach might suffice, or when computational resources for running external panoptic segmentation models and the NeRF-RCNN are limited.
 
 ## Background
-Instance-NeRF was introduced by Yichen Liu, Benran Hu, Junkai Huang, Yu-Wing Tai, and Chi-Keung Tang from The Hong Kong University of Science and Technology, Carnegie Mellon University, and Dartmouth College [S1]. It was published in 2023 [S1]. The work is one of the first significant attempts to perform 3D instance segmentation in NeRF without using ground-truth segmentation as input [S1]. It builds upon NeRF-RPN [S1] and addresses the limitations of Semantic-NeRF and Panoptic NeRF regarding multi-view inconsistency and overlapping objects [S1].
+Instance-NeRF was proposed by Yichen Liu, Benran Hu, Junkai Huang, Yu-Wing Tai, and Chi-Keung Tang from The Hong Kong University of Science and Technology, Carnegie Mellon University, and Dartmouth College [S1]. It was presented at ICCV 2023 [S1]. The work addresses the gap in 3D instance segmentation within NeRF frameworks, building upon prior work like Semantic-NeRF and Panoptic NeRF, while introducing a novel approach to handle instance ID inconsistency without 3D priors [S1].
 
